WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.110 --> 00:00:08.000
CHARLES NESSON: People aren't organized.

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:16.630
People don't have a way of learning about citizenship.

00:00:16.630 --> 00:00:23.750
If you take the core of this ideal, to be the citizen-- we the people--

00:00:23.750 --> 00:00:34.400
and you think in legal terms of, how do we legally express our citizenship,

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:38.740
we have very little means, in a way.

00:00:38.740 --> 00:00:40.330
We vote, it's true.

00:00:40.330 --> 00:00:45.510
But our vote is just one drop in an ocean.

00:00:45.510 --> 00:00:52.920
We don't really feel a sense of control through our individual votes.

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:57.580
But jury-- now, jury is something different.

00:00:57.580 --> 00:01:02.130
It's not something that we're doing all the time.

00:01:02.130 --> 00:01:06.820
But when we are called upon to serve on a jury,

00:01:06.820 --> 00:01:14.540
then we are doing something serious-- and actually, the only job

00:01:14.540 --> 00:01:19.880
that the Constitution defines for a citizen.

00:01:19.880 --> 00:01:25.260
All the rest that is talked about citizenship

00:01:25.260 --> 00:01:31.840
is what the state says should be expected of you, as a citizen.

00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:34.560
You are to serve the state.

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:37.980
But no, no-- you turn around from the point of view of we

00:01:37.980 --> 00:01:48.160
the people, trying to frame a state in which we maintain some hold-- the jury,

00:01:48.160 --> 00:01:52.560
that's our opportunity to participate.

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:57.680
So for me, the empowered citizen is the place where

00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:00.780
the Declaration of Independence starts.

00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:05.740
It's expressed in the jury, in our Constitution.

00:02:05.740 --> 00:02:12.400
And we have all we need, even now, to make it work.

